<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Biak-na-Bato</h1><p class="st-meta">1897 CE – 1897 CE · capital Biak-na-Bato</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived revolutionary republic proclaimed by Emilio Aguinaldo in the hills of Bulacan, with the first Filipino constitution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Spanish offensives drove the revolutionaries from Cavite in 1897, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> retreated to the caves of Biak-na-Bato in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-bulacan">San Miguel, Bulacan</a>. There, on 1 November 1897, his followers adopted a provisional constitution drafted by Isabelo Artacho and Felix Ferrer, modelled on a Cuban revolutionary charter.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution created a Supreme Council with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Aguinaldo</a> as president, declared separation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and guaranteed basic rights such as freedom of religion. In practice the republic controlled little beyond its mountain base and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands loyal to it, and it lacked money and arms.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Exhausted, both sides accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</a>, mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-paterno">Pedro Paterno</a> in December 1897. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Aguinaldo</a> and other leaders went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in return for payment, ending the republic. Aguinaldo returned in 1898 to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-biak-na-bato.jpg" alt="Republic of Biak-na-Bato" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Artemio Ricarte · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Biak-na-Bato.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1897 CE – 1897 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Biak-na-Bato</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emilio Aguinaldo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tagalog, Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Biak-na-Bato Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=republic-of-biak-na-bato" aria-label="Republic of Biak-na-Bato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8044 1747 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Biak-na-Bato lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8309 1895h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8364 2003h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8364 2003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Philippines. <a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=republic-of-biak-na-bato">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
